The soft phonon modes present in the band structure computed for KB<<MATH_INLINE_373>>H<<MATH_INLINE_374>> at 12~GPa (Figure <<CMD_517>>(a)) hint that quantum nuclear effects and the consequent anharmonicity could potentially be important in renormalizing the frequencies associated with the phonon modes and their corresponding EPC matrix elements~<<CMD_498>>. Indeed, previous calculations on LaBH<<MATH_INLINE_377>>, whose structure, like KB<<MATH_INLINE_378>>H<<MATH_INLINE_379>>, can be derived from the parent metal <<MATH_INLINE_380>>H<<MATH_INLINE_381>> superhydride, is quite susceptible to these effects~<<CMD_499>>. Specifically, it was shown that solving the Schr\"{o}dinger equation related to the Born-Oppenheimer energy surface with the addition of the ionic kinetic energy elongates the B-H distances with a concomitant softening of the phonon modes with hydrogen character. As a result, the phase was predicted to become dynamically unstable at a notably higher pressure than predicted by phonon calculations performed under the harmonic approximation. At the same time, these effects increased the EPC and therefore the computed <<MATH_INLINE_382>>. Similar conclusions have been reached for H<<MATH_INLINE_383>>S~<<CMD_500>> and LaH<<MATH_INLINE_384>>~<<CMD_501>>. 

Therefore, quantum nuclear effects have been studied on the phonon band structure of KB<<MATH_INLINE_385>>H<<MATH_INLINE_386>> at 5, 12 and 20~GPa (Figure S27). As compared to the harmonic phonons, the quantum nature of the nuclei introduces a separation between the various phonon branches with the upper branch undergoing a red shift, while the intermediate optical branch is squeezed into a smaller range of frequencies, in-line with what would be expected for strong anharmonic behavior. These variations are minimal at 20~GPa but become significant by 12~GPa. In the quantum case the instability near the <<MATH_INLINE_387>>-point (Figure S27) is healed, and this phase is predicted to remain dynamically stable to a pressure slightly higher than
5~GPa where another instability, this time close to <<MATH_INLINE_388>>, appears (as illustrated in the inset of Figure S27).
